.TH PUPPETC-COMPILE 1 "June 2026" "puppetc 0.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
puppetc-compile \- Puppet manifest compiler and validator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B puppetc-compile
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] <\fIpuppet_file_or_directory\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B puppetc-compile
is a native Puppet manifest compiler that parses Puppet DSL files, evaluates
manifests, and generates catalogs. It can be used for syntax validation,
catalog compilation, and CI/CD integration.
.PP
When a directory is provided, \fBsite.pp\fR will be loaded from the
\fBmanifests/\fR subdirectory and modules will be loaded from the
\fBmodules/\fR subdirectory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Output the parsed AST (Abstract Syntax Tree) as JSON.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-eval
Evaluate the manifest and compile the catalog.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-catalog
Output the compiled catalog as JSON. Implies \fB\-e\fR.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty
Output the catalog in human-readable format with colors. Implies \fB\-e\fR.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write output to FILE instead of stdout.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-modules " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to modules directory. Default: \fB./modules\fR
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-node " " \fINAME\fR
Execute only the specified node definition. Supports regex node matching.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all\-nodes
Execute all literal node definitions. Regex nodes are skipped.
Useful for CI/CD validation. Top-level statements are executed once for
registration, then re-evaluated per node with that node's facts.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-facts " " \fIFILE\fR
Load facts from a JSON or YAML file. Supports single-node facter output,
the PuppetDB export format, and the multi-node \fBfacts:\fR YAML map used
for \fB\-\-all\-nodes\fR runs.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-template " " \fITITLE\fR
Display the template output for a file resource with the specified title.
.TP
.BR \-D ", " \-\-hiera\-data " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to Hiera data directory. Default: \fB./data\fR
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-summary
Print a validation summary suitable for CI/CD. Implies \fB\-e\fR.
Exit code: 0 = all nodes OK, 1 = errors found.
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-parallel
Process nodes in parallel for faster execution. Requires \fB\-\-all\-nodes\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-strict\-erb
Promote the ERB migration warnings (removed \fB@class::var\fR sugar,
legacy fact reads in templates, structured-fact type drift) from warnings
to errors.
.TP
.BR \-\-puppet8\-strict\-facts
Promote the legacy top-scope fact lint (\fB$hostname\fR, \fB$::osfamily\fR,
\fB${::fqdn}\fR in strings, parameter defaults) from warnings to errors.
Note that \fIevaluated\fR reads of removed legacy facts always fail with
"Unknown variable", matching Puppet 8 \- this flag only hardens the static
lint, which also covers code paths the compile does not reach.
.TP
.BR \-X ", " \-\-dead\-code
Report classes, defines, Ruby types/functions and templates that are declared
in the modules tree but never invoked during a full compile. Implies
\fB\-\-all\-nodes\fR and evaluation mode.
.sp
The tracker inventories every class, define, function and template
declaration under \fB<modules>/*/manifests/\fR, \fB<modules>/*/templates/\fR
and \fB<modules>/*/lib/puppet/{type,functions,parser/functions}/\fR at startup,
then watches the interpreter for each runtime invocation (include / resource
declaration / template() / epp() / function call / type declaration). After
executing every node it prints the declared items that were never reached.
.sp
Items are grouped by kind (Classes, Defines, Ruby types, Puppet functions,
Ruby functions, Templates). For accurate results the facts file should cover
every real node from the matching PuppetDB; otherwise node blocks whose
certname has no matching fact will never fire and their classes will appear
dead even when deployed in production.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose/debug output.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help message and exit.
.SH PUPPET 8 COMPATIBILITY
Puppet 8 semantics are the default and the compatibility checks always run
(the former \fB\-8\fR/\fB\-\-puppet8\fR opt-in flag was removed); the
compiler is meant to reproduce what a real OpenVox/Puppet 8 server accepts
or rejects.
.SS Static checks (lint)
.IP \(bu 2
Legacy top-scope facts, bare or qualified (\fB$hostname\fR,
\fB$::osfamily\fR, \fB${::fqdn}\fR inside strings, parameter defaults),
shadow-aware: a local assignment or parameter of the same name suppresses
the diagnostic. Facts that still exist under Puppet 8
(\fBpuppetversion\fR, \fBclientcert\fR, ...) get softer wording.
Lazily-loaded module manifests are linted once per file.
.IP \(bu 2
Removed functions as errors: Hiera 3 (\fBhiera*\fR), stdlib
\fBvalidate_*\fR / \fBis_*\fR; deprecated patterns as warnings.
.IP \(bu 2
Duplicate literal hash keys (fatal in Puppet 8), in the entry program and
in module files.
.IP \(bu 2
ERB templates, scanned when rendered: removed \fB@class::var\fR sugar;
\fBscope['hostname']\fR / \fBscope.lookupvar('::fqdn')\fR legacy reads
(with or without \fB::\fR, shadow-aware); \fB@legacy_fact\fR instance
variables (nil under Facter 5); String methods called on facts that became
Hashes in Facter 4+ (\fBmountpoints.split\fR, ...).
.IP \(bu 2
Ruby provider/function files: removed APIs, Ruby 3.x issues, the
\fBdef foo(arg:, ...)\fR keyword shorthand.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmetadata.json\fR puppet \fBversion_requirement\fR ranges that exclude
Puppet 8, checked when a module is included.
.SS Strict evaluation
Evaluation reproduces Puppet 8 runtime failures rather than warning past
them:
.IP \(bu 2
Reading a removed legacy fact as a variable fails with
\fBUnknown variable\fR (manifests) or \fBUndefined variable\fR (ERB
\fBscope[...]\fR), even when Facter still emits the fact. Survivors and
genuinely assigned variables of the same name keep resolving;
\fBscope.lookupvar\fR keeps returning nil (the defensive API).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBscope['class::var']\fR on an undefined class variable raises, so a
defensive \fB<%% if scope[X] %%>\fR fails exactly like the real server;
\fBscope.exist?\fR is the sanctioned probe.
.IP \(bu 2
Duplicate hash keys are also caught when they only collide after string
interpolation.
.IP \(bu 2
Class/define/function parameters and function arguments are checked against
their declared types (String[N], Enum, Pattern, Variant, Optional, and
user-defined type aliases such as \fBStdlib::Fqdn\fR loaded from
\fBmodules/*/types/\fR). Ruby \fB\\A\fR/\fB\\z\fR regex anchors are
translated for the matcher.
.IP \(bu 2
Automatic Parameter Lookup consults global Hiera and the module data layer
(\fBmodules/<mod>/hiera.yaml\fR v5 + \fBdata/\fR with
\fB%{facts.x.y}\fR interpolation), so e.g. \fBapt::ppa_options\fR
resolves as on a real server.
.IP \(bu 2
Built-ins commonly hit during migrations are implemented natively:
\fBfact('os.distro.codename')\fR, user-defined \fBfunction\fR
definitions, \fBdefined(File['x'])\fR, method-call dispatch, multi-title
resource references.
.SH NODE EXECUTION
By default, only the \fBdefault\fR node is executed. Use \fB\-\-node\fR to
execute a specific node (supports regex matching), or \fB\-\-all\-nodes\fR
to execute all literal node definitions.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (or all nodes validated successfully in CI mode)
.TP
.B 1
Error (syntax error, compilation failure, or validation errors in CI mode)
.SH EXAMPLES
Validate a manifest file:
.PP
.RS
puppetc-compile \-e site.pp
.RE
.PP
Compile a catalog for a specific node:
.PP
.RS
puppetc-compile \-c \-n webserver1 /etc/puppet
.RE
.PP
Validate all nodes for CI:
.PP
.RS
puppetc-compile \-\-all\-nodes \-\-summary /etc/puppet
.RE
.PP
Output catalog as JSON:
.PP
.RS
puppetc-compile \-c \-o catalog.json /etc/puppet
.RE
.PP
Migration audit with the lint promoted to errors:
.PP
.RS
puppetc-compile \-a \-P \-s \-\-puppet8\-strict\-facts \-f allfacts.yaml /etc/puppet
.RE
.PP
Report dead code in a modules tree (needs complete facts for full coverage):
.PP
.RS
puppetc-compile \-X \-P \-f allfacts.yaml \-m ./modules \-D ./hieradata manifests/site.pp
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I manifests/site.pp
Main manifest file when using directory mode.
.TP
.I modules/
Modules directory containing Puppet modules.
.TP
.I data/
Default Hiera data directory.
.TP
.I modules/<module>/hiera.yaml, modules/<module>/data/
Module data layer (Hiera v5, \fBdata_hash: yaml_data\fR with
\fBpath\fR/\fBpaths\fR and \fB%{facts.x.y}\fR interpolation), consulted
by Automatic Parameter Lookup after the global hierarchy. Other backends are
skipped with a warning.
.TP
.I .puppetc-policy.json
Optional per-tree resource policy at the tree root. Entries under
\fBdeprecated_resources\fR flag resource declarations by \fBtype\fR and
exact \fBtitle\fR or POSIX-ERE \fBtitle_pattern\fR, with an optional
\fBreason\fR and \fBlevel\fR (\fBwarning\fR, the default, or
\fBerror\fR). Example:
.sp
.RS
{ "deprecated_resources": [
    { "type": "apt::source", "title_pattern": "^openvox[0-7]$",
      "reason": "this branch targets OpenVox 8", "level": "error" } ] }
.RE
.sp
Absent file disables the feature. One diagnostic per resource per run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR puppetc-server (1),
.BR puppetc-agent (1),
.BR puppet.conf (5)
